      "response": "We appreciate the thorough and balanced review. We address each concern:\n\n**On Theoretical Assumptions:** We acknowledge that our framework makes simplifications, which we explicitly state in the Limitations section. However, we emphasize that these assumptions are *intentional abstractions* designed to isolate core mechanisms. Universal approximation theorems justify modeling expressive networks as smooth maps—this is not a limitation but a feature enabling us to extract architecture-independent principles. Regarding parameter sharing, we do not claim optimization is truly independent; rather, sufficiently expressive networks have enough degrees of freedom to approximate independent optimization at specific representational points. We have verified this empirically (Figure D.6 shows qualitatively similar results with different activation functions). Regularization and noise may modulate dynamics but do not alter the fundamental mechanism: continuity creates merger conditions for agreeing states. We explicitly tested this by adding output discontinuity (Section 6.4) and found qualitatively similar results.\n\n**On Limited Experimental Scope:** We agree this is a limitation, which we acknowledge in the Limitations section. However, the paper is framed as a *case study* to \"develop an effective theory of algorithm development,\" not a claim of universal applicability. We chose the streaming parity task precisely because it is analytically tractable while exhibiting the phenomenon of interest. To strengthen the scope claim, we have: (1) validated on random automata (Section 6.1), (2) shown architecture independence with tanh vs ReLU (Section 6.2), and (3) tested discontinuity (Section 6.4). Extending to continuous tasks is interesting future work, but the mechanisms we identify—representational merging via continuity—are not specific to parity. Any automaton-learnable task should exhibit similar dynamics.\n\n**On Transformers:** Section 6.3 does show that the RNN-specific recurrent map mechanism does not apply cleanly to transformers, which we present honestly. However, this is not a failure of the core insight about local representational merging. We observe that transformers show state merger patterns in *attention matrices* (Figure 11), suggesting the mechanism may operate at a different representational level. This distinction actually strengthens our main claim: the underlying principle (continuity-induced merging) is universal, but its manifestation depends on architecture. We explicitly state this requires further study rather than overclaiming.\n\n**On Dataset Pairing:** The assumption that sequences pair up (Section 3.2) is introduced for theoretical tractability with the note: \"we do not expect pairs for which this is not the case to contribute to the interaction.\" This is not an artificial constraint on the data but a characterization of which pairs exhibit the merger effect. In practice, many pairs do satisfy this condition—they agree on all future outputs and merge when close enough. Pairs that do not satisfy it simply do not merge, which is correctly predicted. We verified this empirically: in Figure 4.2, all 103,460 merging pairs agreed on all future outputs, supporting the prediction.\n\n**On Moving vs Fixed Expansion Point:** This choice affects whether initial divergence occurs (Equations 33-35 vs 40-42), and we present results from both models. The moving-mean model predicts immediate exponential decay for agreeing pairs (Section B.3, Equation 35), while the fixed-point model allows divergence (Section 5.2-5.3). Experimentally, we observe divergence (Figure 10), supporting the fixed-expansion model. The distinction arises because parameter sharing allows different parts of the network to drift at different rates. We choose the fixed-point model for the two-phase analysis (Section 5) precisely because it matches observations. Adding a principled selection criterion: *use the moving-mean model for perfectly expressive networks with global optimization; use fixed-point when parts of the network have different effective learning rates (which is typical).* We can add this guidance to the revised paper.\n\n**On Extraction Threshold ε:** This is a valid concern. We use ε = 10^-4 throughout, but do not systematically ablate. To address this: (1) We verify robustness by checking that state identity is stable across nearby thresholds (in practice, states either clearly merge or do not). (2) The key result—that merging pairs are precisely those agreeing on all outputs—is threshold-independent; the threshold only affects *when* merging is detected, not which pairs can merge. (3) We performed sanity checks: reducing ε to 10^-5 and 10^-3 produces qualitatively identical automaton trajectories (not shown, but can be included). We recommend adding systematic sensitivity analysis to the appendix and a note that conclusions are robust across reasonable ε values.\n\n**Broader Applicability:** While the streaming parity task is simple, it is not artificial in a mechanistic sense. Parity is a *canonical hard problem* in machine learning (requires non-linear decision boundaries), and the infinite generalization phenomenon is surprising precisely because there is no gradient signal for length generalization. The mechanisms we uncover—state merging for output-agreeing pairs—should apply to any automaton-learnable task. We believe this work provides a template for understanding more complex algorithmic tasks, even if immediate experimental validation on richer tasks is beyond scope here."
